\subsection{Experiment on Nested WAIT Algorithm}
Next, we evaluate Nested WAIT with unknown output lengths on synthetic and real-world datasets, using matched batch size limits and thresholds proportional to segment arrival rates.

Synthetic dataset: \( m=4 \), prefill=10 tokens, decode=(20, 40, 80, 160), arrival ratio 20:40:80:160, threshold ratio 15:14:12:8. Figure \ref{fig:comparison Nested WAIT, short prefill} shows improved throughput and latency.

Real-world dataset \citep{zheng2023lmsys}: 210,000+ prompts from Vicuna/Chatbot Arena. We sample 50,000 prompts with Poisson arrivals (Figure \ref{fig:distribution} shows length distribution). We group decode lengths into 10 bins of 50 tokens each, compute mean prefill ($\approx 60 tokens, Figure \ref{fig:group}), set \( m=10 \), threshold ratio 66:43:32:24:17:11:7:4:2:1 (Equation~\eqref{eq:nested_wait_thresholds}), and assign decode stage \( k \) to segment \( \lceil k/50 \rceil \).
% More discussions on this segment design are left in Appendix \ref{sec:extension}.

Synthetic workload test: prefill=60, decode types=(50, 100,..., 500), matching real dataset arrival rates, QPS={55, 550} (Figure \ref{fig:comparison_Nested_WAIT_real_world_low}, \ref{fig:comparison Nested WAIT, real world, higher rate}). Nested WAIT outperforms benchmarks.

% \gan{Maybe, we can say that the Figure \ref{fig:comparison_Nested_WAIT_real_world_low} and Figure \ref{fig:comparison Nested WAIT, real world, higher rate} are on static arrival rate, and Figure \ref{fig:comparison Nested WAIT, real world} is on time-varying arrival rate bt with static threshold, which leads to a little latency overflow.}

Real dataset test with original lengths and same thresholds (Figure \ref{fig:comparison Nested WAIT, real world}): Nested WAIT achieves higher throughput with modest latency increase ($\approx 0.5s per prompt) due to static thresholds applied to time-varying arrivals.

